-- title:
--   Q Bipartite Linear Cons Defect Option Eq Sum Some Complement
-- statement:
--   **An option-valued linear consistency defect expands over genuine outcomes.**
--
--   Let $\psi$ be a bipartite quantum state, and let $A$ and $B$ be submeasurements with common outcome set $\operatorname{Option}(\alpha)$ on the two registers. Write $A_a$ and $B_a$ for their outcome operators. Assume that the total operator of $B$ is the identity and that both failure outcomes have zero operator:
--
--   $$
--   B_{\mathrm{total}}=I,
--   \qquad A_{\bot}=0,
--   \qquad B_{\bot}=0.
--   $$
--
--   Define the linear defect by
--
--   $$
--   \Lambda_{\psi}(A,B)=\operatorname{ev}_{\psi}(A_{\mathrm{total}}\otimes B_{\mathrm{total}})-\sum_{o\in\operatorname{Option}(\alpha)}\operatorname{ev}_{\psi}(A_o\otimes B_o).
--   $$
--
--   Then
--
--   $$
--   \Lambda_{\psi}(A,B)=\sum_{a\in\alpha}\operatorname{ev}_{\psi}\!\left(A_a\otimes(I-B_a)\right).
--   $$
--
--   The identity rewrites the total-minus-matching expression as a sum over the genuine `some` outcomes. It is the bookkeeping step that converts the line-one-point consistency comparison into the scalar residual used by the two Cauchy-Schwarz moves.
--
--   Formalization credit: Sirui Lu, Ruixuan Deng, Yanqiao Zhu, and Zhengfeng Ji (arXiv:2609.19814). Mathematical source: Zhengfeng Ji, Anand Natarajan, Thomas Vidick, John Wright, and Henry Yuen (arXiv:2009.12982).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The source declaration is `MIPStarRE.LDT.Pasting.qBipartiteLinearConsDefect_option_eq_sum_some_complement`; its Lean statement gives the exact quantifier order and all boundary hypotheses.
